**Parallelizing multi-head attention** In multi-head attention, as with self-attention, the input and output have the model dimension $d ,$ the key and query embeddings have dimensionality $d _ { k } ,$ and the value embeddings are of dimensionality $d _ { 
u }$ (again, in the original transformer paper $d _ { k } = d _ { v } = 6 4 ,   A = 8 ,$ and $d = 5 1 2 )$ . Thus for each head $c ,$ we have weight layers $\mathsf { W } ^ { \mathsf { Q } } ,$ of shape $[ d 	imes d _ { k } ] , \mathsf { W } ^ { \mathsf { K } } { } _ { c }$ of shape $[ d 	imes d _ { k } ] ,$ and $\mathbf { W } _ { \mathrm { ~ c ~ } } ^ { \mathrm { v } }$ of shape $[ d 	imes d _ { v } ]$ , and these get multiplied by the inputs packed into X to produce Q of shape $\left[ N 	imes d _ { k } ight]$ , K of shape $[ N 	imes d _ { k } ]$ , and V of shape $[ N 	imes d _ { 
u } ]$ . The output of each of the A heads is of shape $[ N 	imes d _ { 
u } ]$ , and so the output of the multi-head layer with A heads consists of A matrices of shape $[ N 	imes d _ { 
u } ]$ To make use of these matrices in further processing, they are concatenated to produce a single output with dimensionality $[ N 	imes A d _ { 
u } ]$ . Finally, we use a final linear projection $ ar { w } ^ { 0 }$ of shape $[ A d _ { 
u } 	imes d ]$ , that reshapes it to the original output dimension for each token.
